WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:06.310
HBO's fantasy epic "Game of Thrones" had very
few happy endings to offer, killing beloved

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:10.469
characters left and right and upsetting fans
in the process — allegedly, George R.R.

00:00:10.469 --> 00:00:15.699
Martin approved those character arcs, or at
the least didn’t overrule them, but still,

00:00:15.699 --> 00:00:18.110
fans didn’t get the ending they wanted.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:21.689
You can have your least favorite character
ending, but certainly if you say that one

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:26.130
of the worst endings actually went to Daenerys
Targaryen right at the end of the show, you’d

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.339
be hard pressed to find disagreement.

00:00:28.339 --> 00:00:32.540
Played by English actress Emilia Clarke, the
icy-haired, dragon-riding daughter of the

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.820
Mad King Aerys II Targaryen had one of the
most epic character turns in TV history.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.780
Early on, Dany appeared to be the ultimate
Big Good for the show: She's headstrong, liberates

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:48.090
cities and takes care of others, faces hardships
and comes out of them stronger than before.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:52.320
At the time, she also seemed to have the most
legitimate claim to rule the Seven Kingdoms.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.230
But in the penultimate episode of Game of
Thrones and its controversial finale, Daenerys

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.120
does a full 180.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.350
She and her followers — including her nephew-slash-lover
Jon Snow — storm King's Landing, where Dany

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.520
uses her fire-breathing dragon to kill thousands
and destroy everything in her path.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.300
Daenerys' series-long protagonist arc ends
in a hard-heel turn — and, ultimately, in

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.470
death, courtesy Jon Snow.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:21.000
On one hand, having one of your most powerful
and beloved characters abruptly descend into

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.659
madness and break bad is par for the course
for "Game of Thrones."

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:28.830
Nevertheless, many fans feel that the arc
was badly handled, and Daenerys' ultimate

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:32.650
fate was simply one of the things that went
wrong with "Game of Thrones."

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:37.140
As such, it's interesting to hear just what
Clarke herself thinks of the character's ultimate

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.370
fate — and, as it happens, she recently
shared some of her views on the subject.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:46.909
During an appearance on MTV News correspondent
Josh Horowitz's podcast, Happy Sad Confused,

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:50.220
Emilia Clarke shared her views on the subject
of the "Game of Thrones" finale.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:54.740
It looks like she fully understands the criticism,
but noted that her job as an actor requires

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:59.470
her to be "on the same page" with the series'
creators and writers — regardless of how

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:00.470
she might feel.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:01.810
"I get why people were pissed.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.460
I totally get it."

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:07.420
She then opened up about her personal point
of view, noting that she'd portrayed Daenerys

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:11.620
for eight seasons, so she was committed to
play her well until the very end.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:12.620
Clarke said,

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:16.480
"Me being the actor, you can't do justice
to the character that you've poured your blood,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.290
sweat, and tears into for a decade without
getting on the same page [with the creative

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:22.829
decisions the team has made].

00:02:22.829 --> 00:02:25.750
So, like, I'm not going to be there being,
like, 'Pfft!

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.379
Fine, I'll do the scene, whatever.'

00:02:27.379 --> 00:02:28.469
You have to turn up.

00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:29.469
Because why?

00:02:29.469 --> 00:02:32.769
Because you're doing it for you, and you're
doing it for her, and you're doing it for

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:35.790
the show, and you're doing it for the storytelling."

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:40.819
Clarke added that while she does "have a certain
autonomy over what flavor" she can bring to

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:45.430
the story she's tasked with telling, but the
end product is ultimately out of her hands.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.989
"The editor decides what it looks like, and
the writer decides what I'm saying.

00:02:48.989 --> 00:02:53.900
So you just gotta go in and try and give it
as much truth and honesty ... and yourself

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.680
as you possibly can."

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.669
When Horowitz asked whether Clarke had suspicions
that people would be upset over "Game of Thrones"

00:03:00.669 --> 00:03:04.330
finale when she first saw the Season 8 scripts,
the actress said,

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:09.090
"Well, the first instinct when you read the
scripts is you just get that gut punch of

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.799
like, 'Whoa, okay!'

00:03:10.799 --> 00:03:13.099
You're thinking about — your first reaction
is that.

00:03:13.099 --> 00:03:14.159
It's 'okay.'

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:15.849
And then you start to go, 'Oh.

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:19.439
Maybe people are going to ... mmm, yeah.'"

00:03:19.439 --> 00:03:23.939
Clarke then admitted that she "wondered" whether
people might enjoy the events of the series

00:03:23.939 --> 00:03:27.879
finale, and revealed that she hadn't looked
at any reviews for the show for the entire

00:03:27.879 --> 00:03:32.549
time she was on it, nor any fan receptions
for plot points, so she wasn't about to start

00:03:32.549 --> 00:03:33.779
after the finale aired.

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:36.309
“YOU’RE A BIT LATE FOR THAT!”

00:03:36.309 --> 00:03:40.390
Regardless, Clarke did take a very diplomatic
approach to the situation.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:44.090
One can certainly try to read between the
lines on her meaning,

